     9	
    10	                         by Nguyen Van Chau (Hanoi)
    11	
    12	    Abstract. We study the behavior at infinity of non-zero constant Jacobian polyno-
    13	mial maps f = (P, Q) in C2 by analyzing the influence of the Jacobian condition on the
    14	structure of Newton–Puiseux expansions of branches at infinity of level sets of the com-
    15	ponents. One of the results obtained states that the Jacobian conjecture in C2 is true if
    16	the Jacobian condition ensures that the restriction of Q to the curve P = 0 has only one
    17	pole.
    18	
    19	
    20	     1. Introduction. Let f = (P, Q) be a polynomial mapping of C2 into
    21	itself, P, Q ∈ C[x, y], and denote by J(P, Q) := Px Qy − Py Qx the Jacobian
    22	of f . The Jacobian conjecture in C2 (JC2 ), first posed by Keller [K] in 1939
    23	and still open, asserts that a polynomial map f = (P, Q) is an automorphism
    24	of C2 if J(P, Q) ≡ const 6= 0. We refer the readers to [BCW] and [D2] for the
    25	history of the conjecture and related topics, and to [Ka], [H], [LW], [O1–O3],
    26	[P] and [St] for some recent partial results on (JC2 ).
    27	     In this paper we study the behavior at infinity of polynomial maps f =
    28	(P, Q) of C2 satisfying the Jacobian condition J(P, Q) ≡ const 6= 0. First
    29	we try to analyse the influence of the Jacobian condition on the structure
    30	of Newton–Puiseux expansions of branches at infinity of level sets of P and
    31	Q. Then, by applying standard results on the topology of plane curves we
    32	give some estimates on the geometric degree and branched value set of f
    33	and the topology of the generic fibers of the components P and Q.
    34	     Denote by deggeo f := max{#f −1 (a) : a ∈ C2 } the geometric degree of
    35	f , and by χP and χQ the Euler–Poincaré characteristics of the generic fibers
    36	of P and Q, respectively.
    37	     Our main results can be stated as follows.
    38	
    39	    1991 Mathematics Subject Classification: 14H09, 14E40.
    40	    Key words and phrases: Jacobian conjecture, polynomial automorphism, Newton–
    41	Puiseux expansion.
    42	    Research supported in part by the National Basic Program on Natural Science, Viet-
    43	nam, CNRS, ICTP, IMPA and TWAS.
