    21	   The well-known Jacobian Conjecture states that such a mapping is polynomially
    22	invertible (surveys on this subject may be found in [1] and [2]). Polynomial invert-
    23	ibility of f satisfying (1) is obviously equivalent to the fact that a generic point of
    24	C2 has at most one preimage (see, for example [2], Theorem 2.1). We shall call the
    25	number of preimages of a generic point the multiplicity of the mapping.
    26	   The main result of this paper is
    27	Theorem 1.1. The Jacobian of a two-sheeted or three-sheeted polynomial mapping
    28	e 2 → C2 vanishes.
    29	C
    30	   In other words, the multiplicity N of the above mapping cannot equal to two or
    31	three. The fact that N 6= 2 is well-known (see, for example, [2], Theorem 2.1, (a)
    32	⇔ (g)), and it follows immediately from the following arguments. If we compactify
    33	e 2 and C2 , and then, using σ-processes (blowups) resolve all the indeterminacy
    34	C
    35	points of the obtained rational mapping, then we get a branched finite covering f˜
    36	of some projective surface X  e over CP 2 such that the preimage of C2 contains C   e2
    37	as a Zariski open subset. The image of the branching curve is an algebraic curve.
    38	Let us denote it by K. This already implies that the mapping cannot be two-fold.
    39	Indeed, if it were then f˜−1 (K) ∩ C
    40	                                   e 2 = ∅, hence, the variety f˜−1 (C2 − K) would be
    41	simply connected (since it contains C  e 2 ) and would be a finite unbranched covering
    42	of C2 −K. This is impossible because the fundamental group π1 (C2 −K) is infinite.
    43	   This kind of purely topological arguments does not extend even to the three-fold
    44	case. Vitushkin [3] constructed an example of a three-fold simplicial (not analytic)
    45	branched covering of C2 satisfying all the above conditions except the condition
