    68	Jacobian condition and the non-proper value set*, Ann. Polon. Math. 84 (2004),
    69	203--210, DOI `10.4064/ap84-3-2`, Theorem 1 and Corollary 2; the official IMPAN
    70	PDF and arXiv `math/0305088` contain the same statements.
    71	
    72	## 2. Repair of the polynomial-parametrization dependency
    73	
    74	Let
    75	
    76	```text
    77	A2 --g1--> Y --pi=g2--> A2,        deg(pi)=4,
    78	R=NonEt_Y(pi)_red,                 B=pi(R)_red,
    79	U=Y-R.
    80	```
    81	
    82	The block structure theorem gives `B subset A_F`, where `A_F` is the
    83	nonproper-value curve of the original Keller map.  Since `pi` is finite, the
    84	image of every irreducible component of `R` is a closed irreducible curve.
    85	Thus every irreducible component `B_i` of `B` is contained in an irreducible
    86	component of `A_F` of the same dimension and is equal to it.
    87	
    88	Chau Theorem 1 gives a nonconstant polynomial parametrization
    89	`A1 -> B_i`.  It lifts to the normalization and extends to a nonconstant map
    90	from `P1` to the smooth projective normalization.  Luroth gives genus zero.
    91	All finite points of the source map into the affine curve, so every puncture
    92	of the affine normalization lies under the single point at infinity of the
    93	source `P1`; surjectivity allows at most one puncture and affineness requires
    94	one.  Therefore
    95	
    96	```text
    97	normalization(B_i) = A1.                                  (2.1)
    98	```
    99	
   100	The generic branch partition is `(2,1,1)` or `(3,1)`, with one ramification
   101	point.  The corresponding component of `R` is birational to `B_i`, so its
   102	normalization is also `A1`.  This supplies the one-place input that the
   103	quartic producer used without charging.  No assertion that `B=A_F`, or that
   104	the whole reducible curve has one analytic branch at infinity, is made.
   105	
   106	## 3. Correct source-forest and quotient-graph statement
   107	
   108	The morphic forest theorem concerns a resolved strict-SNC boundary.  On the
   109	affine ramification curve it permits several components to meet at one point:
   110	after blowing up, the configuration may be a star.  It permits unibranch
   111	cusps as well.  What it forbids is a cycle in the component/singular-point
   112	incidence graph, including a self-node or two distinct intersections between
   113	the same components.
   114	
   115	With (2.1), each connected component of `R` is consequently a tree of
   116	contractible pieces and is contractible.  Put `h=b0(R)`.  The finite map
   117	`R->B` is point-bijective except that a `(2,2)` fibre identifies one pair of
   118	distinct source points.  Hence `B` is homotopy equivalent to the graph formed
   119	from `h` vertices by adding `n22` edges.  If `k=b0(B)`, then
   120	
   121	```text
   122	e(B)=h-n22,
   123	beta=b1(B)=n22-h+k.                                    (3.1)
   124	```
   125	
   126	This graph is the quotient graph, not the naive pairwise-intersection graph
   127	of unrevolved affine components.
   128	
   129	## 4. Every rank-four survivor has a branch cycle
   130	
   131	The producer proves that a connected simply connected `B` forces
   132	`e_c(U)<=0`, contradicting the promoted ruling identity
   133	
   134	```text
   135	e_c(U)=e(C)+Q >= 1,       C in {A1,P1}, Q>=0.           (4.1)
   136	```
   137	
   138	The review closes the disconnected case.  If every connected component of
   139	`B` is simply connected and `B` is disconnected, Arzhantsev--Zaidenberg
   140	Corollary 1.2 makes it a union of `r>=2` parallel lines.  On each line the
   141	producer's Cohen--Macaulay specialization inequality gives at most two
   142	unramified sheets at every point.  Therefore
   143	
   144	```text
   145	e_c(U) <= 4(1-r)+2r = 4-2r <= 0,                       (4.2)
   146	```
   147	
   148	again contradicting (4.1).  Thus some connected component of `B` has a cycle:
   149	
   150	```text
   151	beta=b1(B)>=1.                                         (4.3)
   152	```
   153	
   154	By (3.1), `n22>=h-k+1>=1`.  In particular every proper rank-four block has a
   155	`(2,2)` conductor fibre; the disconnected acyclic successor proposed in the
   156	producer must not be launched.
   157	
   158	## 5. Monodromy and local-group corrections
   159	
   160	Divisorial inertia is a transposition or a three-cycle.  Meridians normally
   161	generate the global monodromy, excluding the regular `C4,V4` actions and
   162	`D4`, whose transpositions generate only an intransitive order-four subgroup.
   163	If the group were `A4`, all divisorial inertia would be a three-cycle.  A
   164	`(2,2)` local group preserves two pairs and meets `A4` in a group containing
   165	no three-cycle, so `n22=0`.  Then (3.1) makes every branch component
   166	contractible.  The `h=1` row falls to Section 4; for `h>=2`, the exact Euler
   167	ledger gives `e(U)=4-3h-n4<1`.  Hence
   168	
   169	```text
   170	G=S4.                                                   (5.1)
   171	```
   172	
   173	At any closed branch point the plane-curve local complement group is generated
   174	by its branch meridians, not merely normally generated by them.  Therefore a
   175	local decomposition group at a `(4)` fibre must be generated by its own
   176	transpositions and three-cycles.  `D4` fails this test, so only `A4` or `S4`
   177	can occur.  Passing through `S4->S3` on the three pairings yields (0.2).  The
   178	formerly listed `(4),D4 -> (2,1)` row is empty.
   179	
   180	The normalized resolvent is still not a proper cubic block: its field need
   181	not embed in `C(x,y)`, and no etale first leg `A2->Z` is supplied.  Thus the
   182	promoted cubic block theorem cannot be applied to it.  The cover-side cubic
   183	acyclic lemma is also unavailable because (4.3) forces cyclic branch.
   184	
   185	## 6. A global component lower bound
   186	
   187	Let `B_1,...,B_m` be the irreducible components.  Their generic numbers of
   188	unramified sheets are `u_j=2` or `1`.  For a finite set `Sigma` containing all
   189	singular and exceptional branch points, normalization additivity gives
   190	
   191	```text
   192	e_c(U)=4-4e(B)+sum_j u_j
   193	       +sum_(z in Sigma)(u(z)-sum_(branches j at z)u_j). (6.1)
   194	```
   195	
   196	The local orbit/specialization argument makes every bracket nonpositive.  At
   197	each `(2,2)` point at least two transposition-generic branches occur, so its
   198	bracket is at most `-4`.  Using `sum_j u_j<=2m`, (3.1), and (4.1),
   199	
   200	```text
   201	1 <= e(U) <= 4-4(h-n22)+2m-4n22 = 4-4h+2m.
   202	```
   203	
   204	Thus `2m>=4h-3`, equivalently
   205	
   206	```text
   207	m>=2h-1.                                               (6.2)
   208	```
   209	
   210	This is vacuous at the minimal `h=1` row but constrains every multi-component
   211	source survivor.
   212	
   213	## 7. Exact remaining frontier
   214	
   215	The first surviving row is
   216	
   217	```text
   218	h=k=1, beta=n22=1, n4=0.                               (7.1)
   219	```
   220	
   221	The separate threat-map packet shows that only three arithmetic subrows remain:
   222	no finite `(3,1)` values with `(C,Q)=(A1,1)` or `(P1,0)`, and one finite
   223	`(3,1)` value with `(C,Q)=(A1,0)`.  Two active attacks split these into the
   224	infinity-transport and unique-cusp/companion-divisor horns.  The named nodal
   225	cubic is only a topology/control example: its complement has cyclic fundamental
   226	group and cannot itself realize the two disjoint node transpositions, but that
   227	does not classify all one-place `b1=1` curves.
